  "worked_solution": "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={t}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{\u2033}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda -2}+t\\cdot \\lambda {t}^{\\lambda -1}-3{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 2\\lambda (\\lambda -1){t}^{\\lambda }+\\lambda {t}^{\\lambda }-3{t}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {t}^{\\lambda }[2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda -3] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ 2{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(2\\lambda -3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =-1$ and $\\lambda =\\frac{3}{2}.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}{t}^{-1}+{c}_{2}{t}^{3/2}.\n$$",
